--- a/doc/muxers.texi
+++ b/doc/muxers.texi
@@ -1461,6 +1461,42 @@ filter to limit the muxed packet size, because this format does not support
 muxing packets larger than 65535 bytes (3640 samples). For newer ffmpeg
 versions audio is automatically packetized to 36000 byte (2000 sample) packets.
 
+@anchor{dsf}
+@section dsf
+DSD Stream File (DSF) muxer.
+
+It accepts exactly one audio stream with one of the DSD codecs
+(@samp{dsd_lsbf}, @samp{dsd_msbf}, @samp{dsd_lsbf_planar} or
+@samp{dsd_msbf_planar}) and 1 to 6 channels. The audio data is always
+written least significant bit first, in blocks of 4096 bytes per channel;
+the last block of each channel is padded with zeros. The file and data
+sizes are written at the end, so the output should be seekable.
+
+The sample count in the header is 8 times the number of bytes per channel.
+The DSF demuxer drops a partial last byte, so a stream copy of a DSF file
+whose sample count is not a multiple of 8 loses its last 1 to 7 samples.
+
+The channel type is set from the channel layout (mono, stereo, 3.0, quad,
+3.1, 5.0 or 5.1); other layouts get the default type for their channel
+count.
+
+@subsection Options
+@table @option
+@item write_id3v2 @var{bool}
+Write the global metadata and chapters as an ID3v2 tag after the audio
+data. Enabled by default; no tag is written if there is no metadata.
+
+@item id3v2_version @var{integer}
+Select the ID3v2 version to write, 3 or 4 (ID3v2.3 or ID3v2.4). The
+default is 4.
+@end table
+
+@subsection Example
+Copy the DSD audio of a DFF file into a DSF file:
+@example
+ffmpeg -i INPUT.dff -c:a copy OUTPUT.dsf
+@end example
+
 @section dv
 DV (Digital Video) muxer.
 
